{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Maroon Wars''' ({{Lang-bl|Guerras marrón}}) also known '''the''' '''Intifada''' ({{Literal translation|the uprising}}) is a term used in Baliscan historiography to refer to successful {{W|insurrection}}s by {{W|slave revolt|self-liberated slaves}} against [[Colonial Balisca|European colonial rule]] in the Baliscan archipelago. The first revolts began immediately following the introduction of slavery in [[Bayara]] (1526) and ended in 1809 with the destruction of these institutions and the relatively violent expulsion of former facilitators of chattel slavery during the opening years of the [[Baliscan War of Independence|Spanish attempt at reconquest]]. 

In 1521 Spanish colonial authorities, relying heavily on Portuguese slave traffickers, passed a series of ordinances to limit the number of Muslim Africans taken to their colonies in the Americas. Many of these Africans were captives of war with military experience, and they were viewed as particularly dangerous. The Aragonese colonies' desire for slave labor outweighed their fears, which were well-founded as enslaved Africans (Muslims and non-Muslims alike) continued to rebel. Over the next three centuries there would continue to be violent instances of African revolts in the colonies of Bayara, preventing their settlement expansion further into the interior from the coast. 

Collectively, the Maroon Wars became the second and last slave uprising that led to the founding of a state which was both free from {{W|Slavery in the Americas|slavery}} and ruled by non-Europeans and former captives. It is now widely seen as a defining moment in the history of the {{W|Atlantic World}}, particularly in [[W:Latin America|Latin America]].

The early successes of the revolts had a long-lasting impact on the history of the Araucarian territory and the history of Balisca, determining the shape of future colonial relations with self-liberated African and Indigenous peoples, e.g. by causing the consolidation of the Araucarian [[W:Frontier|frontier]].

The effects of these conflicts on the institution of slavery were felt throughout the Americas. The disunity of the {{W|Peninsular War|besieged}} Spanish government, and the ''de facto'' abolition of slavery in the colony was followed by a successful defense of the freedoms the former slaves won and, with the collaboration of already previously {{W|free people of color}}, solidified their independence from white Europeans. 

Together with the simultaneous {{W|Haitian Revolution}}, the revolt in Balisca represented the largest slave uprising since {{W|Third Servile War|Spartacus' unsuccessful}} revolt against the {{W|Roman Republic}} nearly 1,900 years earlier, and challenged long-held European beliefs about slaves' ability to achieve and maintain their own freedom. The rebels' organizational capacity and tenacity under pressure ultimately contributed to the 1809 [[Granyana Proclamation]] which ''{{W|de jure}}'' abolished the practice of slavery across the Baliscan archipelago.

